205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (The human brain did not evolve to function under conditions of continuous uncertainty and perpetual change. Its neurobiological architecture is simply not designed for such an environment. Recognizing that organizations are living systems composed of human brains and nervous systems, it is time to redesign change management by integrating both the realities of the technological era and the principles of how the human brain operates.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (Traditional change management refers to the set of conventional management practices designed to plan, communicate, implement, and reinforce organizational change. Its underlying assumption is that change is a temporary initiative with a clear beginning and end. Consequently, the primary objective is to persuade people to accept change and minimize resistance during implementation. Organizations typically rely on communication campaigns, stakeholder meetings, leadership messages, training programs, and "before-and-after" narratives to support this process.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (These approaches, however, are no longer sufficient for today's environment. They were originally developed to manage discrete, time-bound transformations such as ERP implementations, organizational restructurings, mergers and acquisitions, or the introduction of a new performance management system.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (In today's environment of persistent uncertainty, this paradigm is becoming increasingly inadequate because it fails to account for the brain's threat detection mechanisms, the limits of cognitive capacity, and the adaptive dynamics of the human nervous system. Change is no longer a manageable project with defined start and end points. It has become the permanent operating condition of modern organizations.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (As uncertainty increases, employees are confronted not only with more information but also with greater perceived threat, higher levels of decision fatigue, and more defensive behavioral responses. The field of organizational neuroscience consistently demonstrates that processes such as decision-making, reward, fairness, empathy, learning, and social interaction profoundly influence organizational behavior and performance.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (Sustainable competitive advantage therefore depends on two critical organizational capabilities. The first is designing change processes and organizational systems that are aligned with how the human brain functions. The second is moving beyond managing change toward building decision systems capable of producing higher-quality decisions under conditions of uncertainty.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (From this perspective, Decision Architecture extends far beyond defining roles, authority, and governance structures. It is the deliberate design of organizational systems that enable people to think more clearly under uncertainty, make better decisions, collaborate more effectively, and adapt continuously without overwhelming their cognitive capacity.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 115 0 R
/E (This white paper argues that organizations should stop trying to eliminate uncertainty and instead develop the capability to perform effectively within it. Achieving this requires the integrated design of decision rights, information flows, psychological safety, feedback loops, and learning mechanisms. Together, these elements create an organizational architecture that preserves decision quality even when certainty is no longer available.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
