<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 131 0 R
/T (The foundations of change management were built in a business environment where change was less frequent, more predictable, and where initiatives had clearly defined starting and ending points. Today, however, organizations operate in an environment of continuous disruption driven by technological transformation, artificial intelligence, geopolitical uncertainty, new business models, and ongoing restructuring. )
/E (The foundations of change management were built in a business environment where change was less frequent, more predictable, and where initiatives had clearly defined starting and ending points. Today, however, organizations operate in an environment of continuous disruption driven by technological transformation, artificial intelligence, geopolitical uncertainty, new business models, and ongoing restructuring. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
463 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 131 0 R
/T (Change is no longer a temporary project to be managed; it has become a permanent condition of organizational life.)
/E (Change is no longer a temporary project to be managed; it has become a permanent condition of organizational life.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
464 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 131 0 R
/T (In this new reality, the critical question is no longer how effectively organizations manage change, but how effectively they can make sound decisions under uncertainty. Neuroscience shows us that the brain does not primarily respond to change itself, but to uncertainty. As uncertainty increases, attention narrows, cognitive load rises, threat perception intensifies, and decision quality declines.)
/E (In this new reality, the critical question is no longer how effectively organizations manage change, but how effectively they can make sound decisions under uncertainty. Neuroscience shows us that the brain does not primarily respond to change itself, but to uncertainty. As uncertainty increases, attention narrows, cognitive load rises, threat perception intensifies, and decision quality declines.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
465 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 131 0 R
/T (Therefore, sustainable performance does not come from communicating more, but from designing systems that enable the brain to continue functioning effectively under these conditions.)
/E (Therefore, sustainable performance does not come from communicating more, but from designing systems that enable the brain to continue functioning effectively under these conditions.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
466 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 131 0 R
/T (The competitive advantage of the future will not belong to the organizations that change the fastest; it will belong to those that can preserve their capacity to think, learn, and make decisions under uncertainty. Therefore, the new strategic agenda for organizations should extend beyond change management toward building decision architectures, behavioral design systems, and brain-friendly organizational environments.)
/E (The competitive advantage of the future will not belong to the organizations that change the fastest; it will belong to those that can preserve their capacity to think, learn, and make decisions under uncertainty. Therefore, the new strategic agenda for organizations should extend beyond change management toward building decision architectures, behavioral design systems, and brain-friendly organizational environments.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
